# Flagg fix: typesense snapshot failure, Convex LAN forwarder, MTU drift

Three small infra defects from the 2026-07-31 post-reboot sweep
(`.brain/tasks/reboot-repair-sweep.svx` has the receipts).

## Part 1 — Typesense on-demand snapshot failed

Typesense (`com.joelclaw.central.typesense`, :8108) logged
`On demand snapshot failed, error: copy failed` around 03:00 UTC. Find the
snapshot job (launchd/cron/Inngest — grep the repo and
`/Users/Shared/joelclaw`), find where it copies to, and diagnose the copy
failure (likely: destination NAS volume unmounted during the reboot window,
or a path/permission problem). Fix the root cause if mechanical (e.g. the job
should check the mount before copying), then trigger one on-demand snapshot
and verify it succeeds end to end.

## Part 2 — Convex LAN forwarder absent

`192.168.1.10:3210` (flagg LAN IP) refuses connections while localhost and
tailnet :3210 work. Something used to forward/bind Convex on the LAN address
— find what (grep `192.168.1.10` and `3210` across the repo, esp.
`infra/central/`, `scripts/setup-satellite-rig.sh`, launchd plists, and the
local-convex docs/skills). Determine whether it is still wanted: if a
consumer exists (satellite rig, another machine on the LAN), restore the
forwarder the way it was built; if it was deliberately retired, record that
with the receipt that shows it and remove stale references. Verify with a
real request from the LAN address after.

## Part 3 — MTU drift

`com.joel.mtu-jumbo` sets `en0` MTU 9000; the sweep says the NAS mount
contract expects 8192. Find the contract (grep `8192`/`mtu` in the repo,
`infra/central/scripts/install-nas-mounts.sh`, `mount-nas.sh`, ADRs in
`~/Vault/docs/decisions/`, Brain notes). Decide from the documents which
number is right — do NOT change the live interface MTU on a guess. If the
receipts clearly say 8192, align the mtu-jumbo plist/script AND set the
interface, then verify NAS mounts still respond (`ls /Volumes/badass-media`,
read a file). If the receipts are ambiguous or say 9000 is fine, change
nothing and report the evidence for steering to decide.

## Rules

- Repo edits yes, commits no — steering reviews and commits.
- NAS data is untouchable — snapshot/copy targets only, never delete.
- No destructive commands; dcg BLOCKED = full stop, report.
- Do not touch panda. DONE summary per part: cause, change, verification.

## Result (2026-07-31)

### Part 1 — DONE

- Cause: the backup still assumed Kubernetes. The first native snapshot path
  was not traversable by the `joelclaw` Typesense account. The remote fallback
  passed all SSH flags as one argument and gave `scp` a second incorrect
  `-o`. Same-day retries also reused one snapshot path.
- Change: added native-local snapshot mode, a traversable scratch root,
  inherited `staff` cleanup access, event-unique snapshot paths, correct
  SSH/SCP argument arrays, remote-first transport, and durable transport
  result capture.
- Verification: Inngest run `01KYWWMV1Y0KQBNGFE6JFE5Z03` completed with
  `transportMode: remote`, one attempt, source cleanup `ok: true`, and prune
  `ok: true`. A 4,096-byte read from the NAS copy succeeded.
- Residue: a 52 GB pre-ACL snapshot remains at
  `/tmp/typesense-native-snapshots/20260731`. It was not deleted.

### Part 2 — LIVE, DURABILITY BLOCKED

- Cause: the only LAN forwarder lived in a protected Codex work directory and
  did not return after the reboot. Current satellite and local-Convex docs
  still require the LAN endpoint.
- Change: recreated the forwarder under `infra/central/native/` with a system
  LaunchDaemon template and installer. A detached process now owns the three
  LAN listeners.
- Verification: LAN requests returned HTTP 200 on 3210, the real upstream 404
  on 3211, and HTTP 200 on 6791.
- Blocker: headless user-domain bootstrap returned launchd error 5.
  Non-interactive sudo is unavailable, so the system LaunchDaemon is not yet
  installed.

### Part 3 — LIVE, REBOOT PERSISTENCE BLOCKED

- Cause: receipts prove 8192 as Flagg's safe frame ceiling. Nothing proves
  MTU 9000 end to end.
- Change: active and configured Ethernet MTU is 8192. The tracked plist and
  installer also use 8192.
- Verification: a do-not-fragment 8,164-byte payload reached the NAS. An
  8,972-byte payload failed. The successful Typesense copy and a 4,096-byte
  NAS read verify the LAN/NAS path at MTU 8192.
- Blocker: `/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.joel.mtu-jumbo.plist` still contains
  9000 because non-interactive sudo is unavailable. The existing macOS NFS
  mount also needs a privileged remount before a direct mounted-file read can
  be trusted.
